            <ArticleTitle>Is there a need for another inhalative beta(2)-agonist besides formoterol in patients with asthma?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>432-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Formoterol can substitute the rapid- and short-acting beta(2)-agonists as well as the slow- and long-acting salmeterol. Therefore formoterol in a fixed combination with an inhalant steroid reduces the aerosol devices necessary for asthma control to only one, to be used for regular &quot;controller&quot; and, as needed, &quot;rescue therapy&quot;. The side effect profile of formoterol is comparable to the short-acting beta(2)-agonists which makes the combination with a topically active glucocorticoid applicable in patients of any asthma severity as long as they are able to perform an inspiratory vital capacity maneuver</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Copyright 2001 S. Karger AG, Basel</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
